WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.885
[MUSIC]

00:00:04.885 --> 00:00:15.410
[APPLAUSE]

00:00:15.410 --> 00:00:17.420
&gt;&gt; Well, Mark welcome back to Stanford and

00:00:17.420 --> 00:00:20.430
from one Midwestern to another,
we're honored to have you here.

00:00:20.430 --> 00:00:22.450
&gt;&gt; Great, thank you, thanks everybody.

00:00:22.450 --> 00:00:26.498
&gt;&gt; So, I'd like to begin by exploring
your intense intellectual curiosity,

00:00:26.498 --> 00:00:30.799
recent wired article profiled your library
And instead of it being in your home,

00:00:30.799 --> 00:00:32.909
it's actually in the lobby of A16Z.

00:00:32.909 --> 00:00:33.956
So could you unpack for

00:00:33.956 --> 00:00:37.810
us why you put it in a place open to
visitors as opposed to your home?

00:00:37.810 --> 00:00:40.450
&gt;&gt; Yeah, well, so two reasons, one is
if you've been to other Venture Capital

00:00:40.450 --> 00:00:42.910
offices, they look a little
bit like insurance companies.

00:00:43.920 --> 00:00:47.690
It's all tombstones of long dead
companies that they sold and

00:00:47.690 --> 00:00:49.920
took public once upon a time, so.

00:00:49.920 --> 00:00:52.562
We just thought that was
kind of depressing, so

00:00:52.562 --> 00:00:54.749
we decided to do something different.

00:00:54.749 --> 00:00:58.181
So the message that we're trying
to send with the library, and

00:00:58.181 --> 00:01:01.430
it's a very deliberate message,
kind of goes as follows.

00:01:01.430 --> 00:01:05.120
Which is, the great thing about
the valley, especially in our time,

00:01:05.120 --> 00:01:07.614
is the sense of newness and
the sense of the future.

00:01:07.614 --> 00:01:12.144
And the sense of audacity that
there are radical new ideas and

00:01:12.144 --> 00:01:15.110
that there are new people that come
to the valley all the time and

00:01:15.110 --> 00:01:18.240
can pursue things that have
never been done before.

00:01:18.240 --> 00:01:22.130
So, very future oriented and
that is an enormous strength.

00:01:22.130 --> 00:01:25.658
Elon Musk talks about this,
kind of most vividly, he describes,

00:01:25.658 --> 00:01:28.500
he says you always want to think for
first principles.

00:01:28.500 --> 00:01:31.760
You want to kind of not take any
received wisdom from the past,

00:01:31.760 --> 00:01:36.210
instead you want to think from scratch and
he makes very good method assumptions.

00:01:36.210 --> 00:01:39.440
And the past may no longer be true and
so if you rethink things from scratch,

00:01:39.440 --> 00:01:40.620
you can reach different conclusions and

00:01:40.620 --> 00:01:43.180
do things differently than
could've be done before.

00:01:43.180 --> 00:01:45.590
And I think there's
a huge strength to that,

00:01:45.590 --> 00:01:49.070
I think there's also a big problem with
that, or it's an incomplete theory.

00:01:49.070 --> 00:01:53.690
Which is, there are also thousands of
years of history in which lots and

00:01:53.690 --> 00:01:57.480
lots of very smart people worked very
hard and ran all kinds of experiments

00:01:57.480 --> 00:01:59.740
on inventing new technologies,
or creating new businesses,

00:01:59.740 --> 00:02:03.200
or new ways to manage or
new ways to lead or all kinds of things.

00:02:03.200 --> 00:02:04.781
And they ran all these experiments, and

00:02:04.781 --> 00:02:07.165
they ran these experiments
throughout their entire lives.

00:02:07.165 --> 00:02:10.666
At some point, somebody put them down in
a book, and for very little money and for

00:02:10.666 --> 00:02:11.541
a few hours of time,

00:02:11.541 --> 00:02:15.190
you can literally learn from
somebody's accumulated experience.

00:02:15.190 --> 00:02:17.160
And I think there's just, there is so

00:02:17.160 --> 00:02:21.500
much more to learn from the past
than I think that we often realize.

00:02:21.500 --> 00:02:25.020
And so I think you could productively
spend literally all of your time,

00:02:25.020 --> 00:02:28.840
all your life, just reading the
experiences of great people that have come

00:02:28.840 --> 00:02:31.060
before, and I think you learn every time.

00:02:31.060 --> 00:02:32.340
&gt;&gt; So, speaking of history,

00:02:32.340 --> 00:02:36.690
one of the significant elements in that
library are the history of Hollywood.

00:02:36.690 --> 00:02:39.647
And I'm curious why that fascinates you,
and what parallels, if any,

00:02:39.647 --> 00:02:41.790
you might see to the development
in Silicon Valley.

00:02:41.790 --> 00:02:45.700
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so in a lot of ways,
Hollywood is very unlike the Valley.

00:02:45.700 --> 00:02:48.000
Probably the biggest
different is in the Valley,

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:51.100
if you say that somebody's
startup is just all story.

00:02:51.100 --> 00:02:54.450
And no substance,
it's a very offensive thing to say.

00:02:54.450 --> 00:02:56.720
In Hollywood,
they take that as a huge compliment,

00:02:56.720 --> 00:02:59.540
because the whole point
is to tell a great story.

00:02:59.540 --> 00:03:01.730
And so they view that as a clean win,

00:03:01.730 --> 00:03:06.625
there's another big difference actually
which I find very, very instructive

00:03:06.625 --> 00:03:11.389
to think about which is people in the
valley often think our lives are harder.

00:03:11.389 --> 00:03:16.220
Or it's harder to start these companies or
it's hard to compete for the business.

00:03:16.220 --> 00:03:21.160
Hollywood is much more difficult, it's
a much more actually hardcore competitive,

00:03:21.160 --> 00:03:23.100
even vicious business environment.

00:03:23.100 --> 00:03:26.900
And so it's always good, I think, to have
perspective of people who have it harder

00:03:26.900 --> 00:03:28.330
than we do and
still manage to pull things off.

00:03:28.330 --> 00:03:31.810
I think that's true of the big
successes in Hollywood.

00:03:31.810 --> 00:03:36.350
The thing that they have
in common is we in tech and

00:03:36.350 --> 00:03:40.910
they in entertainment are the two
big original California industries.

00:03:40.910 --> 00:03:45.689
Right, I think between the two industries
we kind of embody the spirit of California

00:03:45.689 --> 00:03:47.720
and the spirit of the West in the US.

00:03:47.720 --> 00:03:51.705
Once upon a time there was a gold rush,
then we pulled out all the gold and

00:03:51.705 --> 00:03:54.480
we're like,
okay now we've gotta make some new gold.

00:03:54.480 --> 00:03:58.070
And we make it through technology and
they make it through storytelling.

00:03:58.070 --> 00:04:02.253
But very similar entrepreneur
personalities, very, lots and

00:04:02.253 --> 00:04:03.930
lots of people.

00:04:03.930 --> 00:04:07.340
Up here everybody's got a start up idea,
down there everybody's got a screen play.

00:04:07.340 --> 00:04:11.710
Lots and lots of people come from
all over the country, migrate in,

00:04:11.710 --> 00:04:14.650
almost everybody's from out of town.

00:04:14.650 --> 00:04:17.963
People have a real sense of what great is,
one of the things about the valley that I

00:04:17.963 --> 00:04:20.448
think is amazing is,
it's impossible to be in the valley and

00:04:20.448 --> 00:04:22.376
not have a sense of what
a great technology is.

00:04:22.376 --> 00:04:26.019
because you have your nose rubbed
in it all the time, it's like yup,

00:04:26.019 --> 00:04:29.860
there's Google, okay,
all right, that's the bar.

00:04:29.860 --> 00:04:34.170
In Hollywood, and for what they do, it's
the same thing, they made everything from

00:04:34.170 --> 00:04:38.230
Casa Blanca to Star Wars all the way
up to all the great movies today.

00:04:38.230 --> 00:04:41.220
And so it's just an incredibly
high quality, high caliber,

00:04:41.220 --> 00:04:42.760
high achieving culture.

00:04:42.760 --> 00:04:46.830
Incredibly energetic people and between
these two industries, California is

00:04:46.830 --> 00:04:49.870
the I think the sixth largest economy
in the world as a stand alone.

00:04:49.870 --> 00:04:54.120
If it were a stand alone country it would
be the sixth largest economy in the world.

00:04:54.120 --> 00:04:57.085
And I think we are the two
industries that power that.

00:04:57.085 --> 00:04:59.686
&gt;&gt; So as you think about the adventure
capitalists you take in a lot of

00:04:59.686 --> 00:05:02.910
information from a lot of different
companies, different trends.

00:05:02.910 --> 00:05:05.885
This seems to be a parallel
to how you approach learning

00:05:05.885 --> 00:05:10.275
taking in a wide variety of history and
other elements from books and stuff.

00:05:10.275 --> 00:05:11.315
How do you think about learning?

00:05:11.315 --> 00:05:15.568
How do you go about actually figuring out
what you want to focus on and, develop?

00:05:15.568 --> 00:05:17.590
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so there's part of what we do, so

00:05:17.590 --> 00:05:21.830
the way modern adventure firms work
is kind of strange and interesting.

00:05:21.830 --> 00:05:23.421
So we get inbound pitches, so

00:05:23.421 --> 00:05:27.890
we get inbound 2000 pitches a year,
from qualified referred entrepreneurs.

00:05:27.890 --> 00:05:32.020
So somebody who's kind of hitting
the basic criteria, have been

00:05:32.020 --> 00:05:35.480
able to network in the Valley, and
been able to connect with someone we know.

00:05:35.480 --> 00:05:40.030
A very qualified set of founders, so
we see 2000 a year, and so a lot of it is

00:05:40.030 --> 00:05:43.790
just meeting with all those founders,
and trying to survive the tsunami.

00:05:43.790 --> 00:05:48.460
I read, there's all these books, there's a
great book, I disagree with the book, but

00:05:48.460 --> 00:05:50.120
it's a very well-written
book called The Rise and

00:05:50.120 --> 00:05:52.850
Fall of American Growth by
an economist named Robert Gordon.

00:05:52.850 --> 00:05:55.420
And he says, basically, innovation is
over, and all the great ideas have

00:05:55.420 --> 00:05:58.970
been taken, and from here on out just
everything's going to get boring.

00:05:58.970 --> 00:06:02.500
There's no new fundamental technological
breakthroughs on the horizon.

00:06:02.500 --> 00:06:07.020
Some days it would be
a relief if that were true,

00:06:07.020 --> 00:06:09.500
because out here,
we're just drowning in it, right.

00:06:09.500 --> 00:06:11.990
It's just this constant
tsunami of new ideas and

00:06:11.990 --> 00:06:15.252
new thinking, and
new people with new thinking.

00:06:15.252 --> 00:06:20.864
And so a lot of it is just being in
that fire hose, that's part of it and

00:06:20.864 --> 00:06:23.822
then like I said, I try to compliment
that trying to be proactive.

00:06:23.822 --> 00:06:27.980
And so, one is trying to explore new areas
of science in particular and there's,

00:06:27.980 --> 00:06:30.390
I think, some very interesting
new areas of science right now.

00:06:30.390 --> 00:06:34.450
Many of which are happening right here at
Stanford, and then the other is history,

00:06:34.450 --> 00:06:35.620
I always go back to history.

00:06:35.620 --> 00:06:37.750
And I always go back to try to understand,
okay,

00:06:37.750 --> 00:06:42.400
when you had a fundamental, I'll give you
an example, self-driving cars, right.

00:06:42.400 --> 00:06:45.710
So self-driving cars are a fundamental
advance in computer technology.

00:06:45.710 --> 00:06:48.803
They're a fundamental advance
in the transportation industry.

00:06:48.803 --> 00:06:52.205
They're obviously going to be a very
big impact on the automotive industry,

00:06:52.205 --> 00:06:56.260
which is a very big global production
industry, consumer industry.

00:06:56.260 --> 00:06:57.750
It's like okay, well what happened,

00:06:57.750 --> 00:06:59.580
the thing about the impact
of self driving cars.

00:06:59.580 --> 00:07:02.121
Well let's start by understanding
what were the impact of cars, right.

00:07:02.121 --> 00:07:05.810
And there were very, very interesting
lessons from how cars rolled out that I

00:07:05.810 --> 00:07:09.044
think can be applied 100 years
later to figuring out some of the,

00:07:09.044 --> 00:07:12.020
both the issues and
the opportunities of self driving cars.

00:07:12.020 --> 00:07:13.440
And so I try to go back and look for

00:07:13.440 --> 00:07:16.350
the patterns that can kind of be more
helpful in predicting the future.

00:07:16.350 --> 00:07:19.330
&gt;&gt; Speaking of science, backstage you
mentioned that at this point in our

00:07:19.330 --> 00:07:24.780
history, 90% of the scientists that have
ever existed and engineers live right now.

00:07:24.780 --> 00:07:26.190
&gt;&gt; Yeah, living or working today.

00:07:26.190 --> 00:07:30.320
It's 90% of all scientists, 80 or 90%,
I think it's 90%, of all scientists and

00:07:30.320 --> 00:07:32.590
engineers, who have ever lived
are living and working today.

00:07:32.590 --> 00:07:35.830
&gt;&gt; So what does that imply for humanity,
going forward the next two decades, and

00:07:35.830 --> 00:07:36.690
even the next century?

00:07:36.690 --> 00:07:40.165
&gt;&gt; Well, this is why I'm such
a gigantic bull on future innovation.

00:07:40.165 --> 00:07:43.360
So there's two basic theories,
the Gordan theory is basically,

00:07:43.360 --> 00:07:45.130
it's a question of low-hanging fruit.

00:07:45.130 --> 00:07:48.470
And so electrical power was low-hanging
fruit, steam engine was low hanging fruit.

00:07:48.470 --> 00:07:51.400
Electromagnetic waves were low-hanging
fruit, and from here on out,

00:07:51.400 --> 00:07:53.220
everything gets much more complex.

00:07:53.220 --> 00:07:56.410
And so it's harder, you're putting more,
diminishing returns.

00:07:56.410 --> 00:07:58.760
You're climbing higher and
higher, and putting more and

00:07:58.760 --> 00:08:00.800
more effort in for less results.

00:08:00.800 --> 00:08:03.868
And actually, by the way, what Gordon
points to that actually justifies that

00:08:03.868 --> 00:08:07.475
point of view is Measured productivity
growth across the economy is quite low.

00:08:07.475 --> 00:08:08.689
Right?
And so, right,

00:08:08.689 --> 00:08:10.865
because basically there's two arguments,

00:08:10.865 --> 00:08:13.812
critics of technology
basically make two arguments.

00:08:13.812 --> 00:08:16.200
One is there's no innovation,
there's no productivity growth.

00:08:16.200 --> 00:08:17.050
And the other is, there's so

00:08:17.050 --> 00:08:19.590
much innovation that it's
going to destroy all the jobs.

00:08:19.590 --> 00:08:22.294
Right, and what's important to realize
is they're diametrically opposed

00:08:22.294 --> 00:08:23.540
arguments, right?

00:08:23.540 --> 00:08:26.140
They literally, factually,
logically can't both be true.

00:08:26.140 --> 00:08:29.207
Either you have super low productivity
growth and nothing changes, right,

00:08:29.207 --> 00:08:32.368
on one side of the argument, or you have
super high productivity growth through

00:08:32.368 --> 00:08:34.323
technological change and
everything changes.

00:08:34.323 --> 00:08:38.840
But they can't be reconciled,
they can't both be true.

00:08:38.840 --> 00:08:41.380
However, there is one way they
actually can both be true,

00:08:41.380 --> 00:08:43.580
which is where things
get really interesting.

00:08:43.580 --> 00:08:45.470
Which is they vary by industry, right?

00:08:45.470 --> 00:08:48.810
And I actually think this is the part
of his argument that I agree with,

00:08:48.810 --> 00:08:53.130
which is that there are industries
like technology, consumer electronics,

00:08:53.130 --> 00:08:55.540
media, retail as examples,

00:08:55.540 --> 00:08:58.710
where there's extremely rapid productivity
growth in technological adoption.

00:08:58.710 --> 00:09:00.490
And then there are other
industries like healthcare,

00:09:00.490 --> 00:09:04.025
education, law, government where there's
a little technological adoption,

00:09:04.025 --> 00:09:05.880
a very little productivity improvement.

00:09:05.880 --> 00:09:08.583
And so one of the things we try to
do is try kind of navigate through

00:09:08.583 --> 00:09:11.900
the differences industry by industry and
try to kind of understand how to apply

00:09:11.900 --> 00:09:16.070
the technology particularly the areas
that where it hasn't been applied before.

00:09:16.070 --> 00:09:18.040
&gt;&gt; To the point of jobs in our economy,

00:09:18.040 --> 00:09:21.450
one of the main criticisms of self-driving
cars in the future is there's 5 million

00:09:21.450 --> 00:09:26.370
truckers all around America that
the role of the economy may diminish.

00:09:26.370 --> 00:09:29.740
We see companies like Facebook and
Twitter compared to their equivalent

00:09:29.740 --> 00:09:34.480
market capitalization companies in the
past decades having far fewer employees.

00:09:34.480 --> 00:09:38.170
So are you of the mindset that
the employment opportunities will actually

00:09:38.170 --> 00:09:42.758
increase as technology proliferates,
and why do you think that?

00:09:42.758 --> 00:09:45.730
&gt;&gt; Also there's an argument from
logic which is sort of the doctrine

00:09:45.730 --> 00:09:47.298
of free trade, specialization of labor,

00:09:47.298 --> 00:09:50.680
it's kind of well established in
economic theory why that would be true.

00:09:50.680 --> 00:09:52.195
Then there's observation by history,

00:09:52.195 --> 00:09:55.269
which as we're sitting here today after
300 years of technological change and

00:09:55.269 --> 00:09:57.464
there are more jobs in the world
than ever before, right?

00:09:57.464 --> 00:09:59.048
There are more jobs in the US
today than ever before,

00:09:59.048 --> 00:10:00.790
there are more jobs around
the world than ever before.

00:10:00.790 --> 00:10:02.610
There are more people who are employed.

00:10:02.610 --> 00:10:04.730
By the way, income levels have
never been higher, right?

00:10:04.730 --> 00:10:07.760
For all of our issues of stagnation of
income, overall income levels across

00:10:07.760 --> 00:10:10.590
the entire population of
the world are at an all-time high.

00:10:10.590 --> 00:10:14.950
And so, if technological change were
going to cause elimination of jobs,

00:10:14.950 --> 00:10:17.220
one presumes we would have seen it by now.

00:10:17.220 --> 00:10:18.060
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
&gt;&gt; Right, again,

00:10:18.060 --> 00:10:21.765
the counter argument to that is but you're
not taking an account like some dramatic

00:10:21.765 --> 00:10:23.680
breakthrough in artificial intelligence.

00:10:23.680 --> 00:10:26.272
At which point I start accusing my
opponents in this argument of just

00:10:26.272 --> 00:10:26.940
hand-waving.

00:10:26.940 --> 00:10:30.850
If it's so easy to make a super genius
AI robot, why don't you give it a shot?

00:10:32.260 --> 00:10:33.800
We are looking to try to fund it,

00:10:33.800 --> 00:10:38.450
in fact come in and pitch us,
we will happily invest.

00:10:38.450 --> 00:10:39.951
It may be the last
investment we ever make, but

00:10:39.951 --> 00:10:41.038
it will be a very successful one.

00:10:41.038 --> 00:10:44.444
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So, we're totally on board with that.

00:10:44.444 --> 00:10:45.358
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:10:45.358 --> 00:10:48.449
&gt;&gt; So, there's sort of
the long run historical view,

00:10:48.449 --> 00:10:51.950
which I think is pretty well established.

00:10:51.950 --> 00:10:53.370
Then the questions get very specific.

00:10:53.370 --> 00:10:56.510
And these are some of the questions that
have come up in this election cycle,

00:10:56.510 --> 00:11:00.220
which is okay, the 5 million people
involved in driving, one form or

00:11:00.220 --> 00:11:03.210
another are transportation jobs in the US,
transportation delivery services,

00:11:03.210 --> 00:11:04.580
trucking, things like that.

00:11:04.580 --> 00:11:07.940
And you're like, wow,
5 million jobs seems like a lot of jobs,

00:11:07.940 --> 00:11:09.929
and it is a lot of jobs.

00:11:11.040 --> 00:11:12.630
This is where I think
people underestimate.

00:11:12.630 --> 00:11:14.970
Even with our low rate of
productivity growth today,

00:11:14.970 --> 00:11:19.400
people underestimate the rate of change
in the economy that happens anyway.

00:11:19.400 --> 00:11:22.940
And the thing that happens there is that
what gets reported are always headline

00:11:22.940 --> 00:11:25.600
numbers and the headline numbers
are always the net numbers.

00:11:25.600 --> 00:11:27.410
And so any given month, they'll report.

00:11:27.410 --> 00:11:28.953
It just came out, I think, two days ago.

00:11:28.953 --> 00:11:33.340
It was last month or I forget, 170,000 new
jobs or something like that in the US.

00:11:33.340 --> 00:11:35.570
And so you're like, jeez,
you're not creating very many jobs.

00:11:35.570 --> 00:11:37.700
How can you possibly create 5 million?

00:11:37.700 --> 00:11:40.290
So that's the net number,
it's not the gross number, right?

00:11:40.290 --> 00:11:41.840
So the gross number is much higher.

00:11:41.840 --> 00:11:45.225
Both in terms of jobs destroyed,
and jobs created.

00:11:45.225 --> 00:11:48.360
And the top line number there,
is every year in the US, on average,

00:11:48.360 --> 00:11:53.100
about 21 million jobs are destroyed, and
about 24.5 million jobs are created, for

00:11:53.100 --> 00:11:55.910
a net add over time of about
3.5 million jobs a year.

00:11:55.910 --> 00:12:00.158
And so the real answer to how do you
replace 5 million jobs is we already

00:12:00.158 --> 00:12:02.469
replaced that in less than a quarter.

00:12:02.469 --> 00:12:05.562
Today, just this quarter today, just this
calendar quarter with the technology we

00:12:05.562 --> 00:12:08.190
already have, we're already going to
create more than 5 million jobs.

00:12:08.190 --> 00:12:09.640
5 million gross new jobs.

00:12:09.640 --> 00:12:12.031
And so change is happening in
the economy all the time, right?

00:12:12.031 --> 00:12:14.684
And by the way, it's happening whether or
not we elect Hillary or

00:12:14.684 --> 00:12:17.819
Trump, it's happening whether or
not we have steel here, somewhere else,

00:12:17.819 --> 00:12:20.400
it's happening whether or
not Silicon Valley rises or falls.

00:12:20.400 --> 00:12:22.907
The change is just going to keep coming.

00:12:22.907 --> 00:12:23.450
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:12:23.450 --> 00:12:27.280
&gt;&gt; And then to me that gets back to
our day job and what I think is so

00:12:27.280 --> 00:12:28.670
important to focus on which is okay.

00:12:28.670 --> 00:12:31.330
So, then it's saying okay,
the world is going to change,

00:12:31.330 --> 00:12:33.170
the jobs are going to change.

00:12:33.170 --> 00:12:36.710
How do we set people up to be able
to take advantage of the change?

00:12:36.710 --> 00:12:38.860
Right?
How do we have change work for people?

00:12:38.860 --> 00:12:40.570
How do we expand opportunities?

00:12:40.570 --> 00:12:41.750
Why education is so important.

00:12:41.750 --> 00:12:44.800
That's why financial services
actually is so important.

00:12:44.800 --> 00:12:47.230
That's why it's so important to be able
to raise money for new businesses.

00:12:47.230 --> 00:12:48.390
That's why it's so important for

00:12:48.390 --> 00:12:51.790
people to be able to move from company
to company to be able to get new jobs.

00:12:51.790 --> 00:12:56.090
And then the conversation I think we ought
to be having is the next hour's worth of

00:12:56.090 --> 00:12:59.690
conversation on all the things that we
could do to have more people have access

00:12:59.690 --> 00:13:02.765
to all the opportunity that the new
technology in many cases is creating.

00:13:02.765 --> 00:13:05.375
&gt;&gt; Yeah, you mentioned Robert Gordon.

00:13:05.375 --> 00:13:09.385
Who else is in your intellectual diet
that you turn to when you want to think

00:13:09.385 --> 00:13:12.625
about deep issues like change and
funding in our economy?

00:13:12.625 --> 00:13:14.987
&gt;&gt; So I have a little mental
model of Peter Thiel.

00:13:14.987 --> 00:13:16.587
I have a little simulation of Peter Thiel.

00:13:16.587 --> 00:13:17.694
He lives on my shoulder right here.

00:13:17.694 --> 00:13:20.645
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And I argue with him all day long.

00:13:20.645 --> 00:13:22.565
&gt;&gt; Who wins?

00:13:22.565 --> 00:13:23.412
&gt;&gt; I always win.

00:13:23.412 --> 00:13:25.205
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Face to face,

00:13:25.205 --> 00:13:28.515
it's slightly more challenging.

00:13:28.515 --> 00:13:30.531
So, I'm a big believer,
goes back to history.

00:13:30.531 --> 00:13:33.730
Charlie Munger talks a lot about
being able to learn from the past.

00:13:33.730 --> 00:13:37.966
He always says, the way he describes it,
basically he calls it a build of mental

00:13:37.966 --> 00:13:41.410
model of a person and
you want to kind of try to understand.

00:13:41.410 --> 00:13:44.508
And it's hard because the people we're
doing this for are very smart people.

00:13:44.508 --> 00:13:48.071
And so you have to try to simulate people
who [LAUGH] in a lot of cases are smarter

00:13:48.071 --> 00:13:48.762
than you are.

00:13:48.762 --> 00:13:51.440
We want to build a mental model, and
that could be a mental model of,

00:13:51.440 --> 00:13:55.602
in modern times, Steve Jobs or Jeff Bezos
or somebody, Larry Page, Elon Musk.

00:13:55.602 --> 00:13:59.100
We have mental model of somebody from
the past, Thomas Edison, J.P. Morgan.

00:13:59.100 --> 00:14:01.180
It can be a model of
just somebody you admire,

00:14:01.180 --> 00:14:03.520
a great philosopher or religious figure.

00:14:03.520 --> 00:14:07.490
And you want to kind of construct
a model of how they think.

00:14:07.490 --> 00:14:09.070
And be able to be very objective and

00:14:09.070 --> 00:14:11.860
fair where you can kind of think
things through from their standpoint.

00:14:11.860 --> 00:14:14.290
And then you kind of you have
your own view on things and

00:14:14.290 --> 00:14:17.160
then you try to run through kind of in
your head what you know of them and

00:14:17.160 --> 00:14:19.870
so okay here are the conclusions
that they would reach.

00:14:19.870 --> 00:14:21.060
And if you put enough time into that,

00:14:21.060 --> 00:14:23.710
you start to be able to have these
conversations I find sort of

00:14:23.710 --> 00:14:24.580
with yourself.

00:14:24.580 --> 00:14:26.850
People might look at you funny
while it's happening but

00:14:26.850 --> 00:14:31.590
you get to kind of engage in this dialogue
and so I'm a big believer in that.

00:14:31.590 --> 00:14:36.404
The three people in the valley who I
do that with most often, my shoulder,

00:14:36.404 --> 00:14:38.666
Peter, Elon Musk and Larry Page.

00:14:38.666 --> 00:14:42.946
The three of them I find pushed the
boundaries of what technology can do and

00:14:42.946 --> 00:14:46.130
what Silicon Valley can do
further than anybody else.

00:14:46.130 --> 00:14:48.024
I think they're the three most
audacious people, I think,

00:14:48.024 --> 00:14:49.443
probably who have ever
worked in the Valley.

00:14:49.443 --> 00:14:50.492
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:14:50.492 --> 00:14:54.300
&gt;&gt; And then you've got your
models of the great founders.

00:14:54.300 --> 00:14:59.553
Steve Jobs, obvious, and many other
folks who built amazing companies

00:14:59.553 --> 00:15:04.280
as entrepreneurs and then the new one for
us is the great CEO's, right?

00:15:04.280 --> 00:15:06.753
So Andy Grove being kind
of our base case for that.

00:15:06.753 --> 00:15:10.027
So, and whenever we talk about management
in our firm, we're always trying to index

00:15:10.027 --> 00:15:12.798
back on okay, what would Andy Grove
say if he were sitting here today.

00:15:12.798 --> 00:15:16.880
Good news that Andy is that he was
very forceful in expressing his views.

00:15:16.880 --> 00:15:22.360
And so he's on the record with
on most of the important topics.

00:15:22.360 --> 00:15:25.775
And as a very good kind of reference
point for thinking about those issues.

00:15:25.775 --> 00:15:28.336
&gt;&gt; What I'm struck by is you love
to engage with people who may

00:15:28.336 --> 00:15:29.230
disagree with you?

00:15:29.230 --> 00:15:29.905
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:15:29.905 --> 00:15:30.855
&gt;&gt; What's the broader lesson here,

00:15:30.855 --> 00:15:34.425
for those of us here in business school
who are thinking about these things?

00:15:34.425 --> 00:15:37.585
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so I just think, and again,
this is the kind of thing where, this way.

00:15:37.585 --> 00:15:40.490
Adventure Capital,
[LAUGH] venture capital.

00:15:40.490 --> 00:15:41.805
Venture capital is a funny business.

00:15:41.805 --> 00:15:43.250
Let me say why I feel so strongly on this.

00:15:43.250 --> 00:15:46.540
So in venture capital, there's two
kinds of mistakes you can make.

00:15:46.540 --> 00:15:48.550
There's a mistake of omission.

00:15:48.550 --> 00:15:49.572
Or there's a mistake of commission.

00:15:49.572 --> 00:15:53.970
Which is mistake most people
make because I make a mistake.

00:15:53.970 --> 00:15:56.810
I make a decision, I invest in a company,
I lose all my money.

00:15:56.810 --> 00:15:58.052
It's the mistake everybody
kind of thinks about.

00:15:58.052 --> 00:15:59.750
And then there's the mistake of omission.

00:15:59.750 --> 00:16:05.741
Which is Mark Zuckerberg walks in the door
at venture capital firm XYZ in 2004.

00:16:05.741 --> 00:16:08.630
And, you know,
like what is this little kid doing?

00:16:08.630 --> 00:16:10.162
And this idea's crazy.

00:16:10.162 --> 00:16:13.294
And Fredster proved that
this could never work.

00:16:13.294 --> 00:16:16.321
And this is ridiculous, which is
what he got told by a lot of people.

00:16:16.321 --> 00:16:18.751
In the venture capital business,

00:16:18.751 --> 00:16:24.830
every highly successful VC has made
mistakes of omission, really big ones.

00:16:24.830 --> 00:16:28.540
Of companies that they had the chance to
invest in, they should've invested in,

00:16:28.540 --> 00:16:29.688
they didn't invest in.

00:16:29.688 --> 00:16:33.952
It turns out the mistakes of Co-Mission,
they matter, but

00:16:33.952 --> 00:16:36.133
they don't scar you for life.

00:16:36.133 --> 00:16:39.623
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; They just fade into history.

00:16:39.623 --> 00:16:41.128
The mistakes of omission, right?

00:16:41.128 --> 00:16:42.332
It's a asymmetric payoff.

00:16:42.332 --> 00:16:45.241
When these companies work, you know,
they get a thousand extra.

00:16:45.241 --> 00:16:49.895
If you lose, you lose 1 x, if you win,
we win 1000 extra, 10,000x.

00:16:49.895 --> 00:16:52.170
The other is,
the companies succeed, right?

00:16:52.170 --> 00:16:54.695
The companies you passed on succeed,
and they torture you, right?

00:16:54.695 --> 00:16:56.440
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Because, you know.

00:16:56.440 --> 00:16:57.189
&gt;&gt; So, who's tortured you recently?

00:16:57.189 --> 00:16:59.762
&gt;&gt; No comment
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:16:59.762 --> 00:17:00.281
&gt;&gt; I can't even bring

00:17:00.281 --> 00:17:00.967
myself to talk about it.

00:17:00.967 --> 00:17:04.953
You pull up Tech Meme in the morning or
whatever your choice of thing is, and

00:17:04.953 --> 00:17:07.438
you're just like, shit, that one again.

00:17:07.438 --> 00:17:09.611
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And so

00:17:09.611 --> 00:17:10.727
they just torment the hell out of you.

00:17:10.727 --> 00:17:16.108
And so, it's a humbling job.

00:17:16.108 --> 00:17:18.860
I think the same basic principle
applies to, but much more broadly,

00:17:18.860 --> 00:17:21.994
I think the same principle, especially for
people kind of in this ecosystem,

00:17:21.994 --> 00:17:24.985
I think the same principle applies to
even people, that mentality applies

00:17:24.985 --> 00:17:28.000
to people with kind of what you consider
to be more normal careers, right?

00:17:28.000 --> 00:17:31.008
So, as you think about having
a career over 50 years,

00:17:31.008 --> 00:17:34.159
you're going to make basically
a sequence of bets, right?

00:17:34.159 --> 00:17:36.555
And you're going to make a sequence of
bets of the places you choose to go and

00:17:36.555 --> 00:17:37.745
the people you choose to work with.

00:17:37.745 --> 00:17:39.830
And you're going to
screw some of those up,

00:17:39.830 --> 00:17:42.631
and you're going to make a series
of bets and decisions for

00:17:42.631 --> 00:17:46.041
places you don't want to go and
people you don't want to work with.

00:17:46.041 --> 00:17:47.737
And you're going to make those
mistakes on both sides, and

00:17:47.737 --> 00:17:50.180
you're going to have the exact
same feeling on the other side.

00:17:50.180 --> 00:17:53.627
When you had the chance to be
employee number four at Google and

00:17:53.627 --> 00:17:55.298
you didn't take it, right?

00:17:55.298 --> 00:17:56.546
That sticks with you for a while.

00:17:56.546 --> 00:17:58.216
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; That's a big one,

00:17:58.216 --> 00:17:59.406
rhat one tends to wear on you.

00:17:59.406 --> 00:18:04.460
And so, I think that mentality, we call
it the slugging percentage mentality.

00:18:04.460 --> 00:18:07.597
Which is basically, take the bat,
lose 1x, don't take the bat,

00:18:07.597 --> 00:18:09.096
possibly miss on 1,000x.

00:18:09.096 --> 00:18:12.250
That mentality is a very
powerful mentality.

00:18:12.250 --> 00:18:15.790
If you're going to do that, you have to
be kind of ruthlessly open-minded, right?

00:18:15.790 --> 00:18:19.572
And then this is sort of my view,
when we make mistakes of omission,

00:18:19.572 --> 00:18:20.458
why do we do it?

00:18:20.458 --> 00:18:24.958
And I think it's almost always because we
have some theory for why something's not

00:18:24.958 --> 00:18:28.620
going to work, and you try to
investigate that theory, but you know,

00:18:28.620 --> 00:18:33.610
the problem with human nature, you develop
a theory and you tend to want to prove it.

00:18:33.610 --> 00:18:35.251
A confirmation bias, they call it.

00:18:35.251 --> 00:18:37.799
And so, you develop an idea,
and then you look for

00:18:37.799 --> 00:18:42.229
all the evidence that supports it, and you
ignore all the evidence that disproves it,

00:18:42.229 --> 00:18:44.610
and so you get locked into your ideas,
right?

00:18:44.610 --> 00:18:46.954
And then by the way,
pattern matching works against you, right?

00:18:46.954 --> 00:18:49.652
Because things that didn't work
in the past, might work now.

00:18:49.652 --> 00:18:52.259
And this is the problem with history,
this is why Elon focuses so much for

00:18:52.259 --> 00:18:53.000
first principles.

00:18:55.180 --> 00:18:58.545
Just because like MySpace, for
example, didn't reach Facebook levels,

00:18:58.545 --> 00:19:01.184
the scale didn't mean that
Facebook wouldn't be able to.

00:19:01.184 --> 00:19:03.584
So, you have to be ruthlessly open-minded,

00:19:03.584 --> 00:19:07.195
you have to be constantly willing
to re-examine your assumptions.

00:19:07.195 --> 00:19:11.961
And to do that you have to try to figure
out a way to not get emotionally tied to

00:19:11.961 --> 00:19:12.957
your beliefs.

00:19:12.957 --> 00:19:16.943
So as I always like to say, there's
this software term called sandbox, and

00:19:16.943 --> 00:19:20.307
there's the idea that you can
basically run code on bare metal,

00:19:20.307 --> 00:19:23.960
you can run code on the chip, or
you can run code on a sandbox.

00:19:23.960 --> 00:19:28.374
The idea of a sandbox is, it's a contained
environment, where if the code goes bad,

00:19:28.374 --> 00:19:31.437
if it's Malware, or
it's an AI that wakes up or whatever,

00:19:31.437 --> 00:19:34.041
you can nuke the sandbox and
nothing bad happens.

00:19:34.041 --> 00:19:37.404
I think we need to run ideas in sandboxes.

00:19:37.404 --> 00:19:39.729
Beliefs should be run in sandboxes.

00:19:39.729 --> 00:19:44.097
As much as possible,
you shouldn't self identify with beliefs.

00:19:44.097 --> 00:19:46.498
We should treat them all as
sort of more abstract objects.

00:19:46.498 --> 00:19:49.661
And be willing to pull them in, think
about them, and then put them back on

00:19:49.661 --> 00:19:52.381
the shelf, as opposed to saying,
this is part of my identity.

00:19:52.381 --> 00:19:54.095
And it's a very hard thing.

00:19:54.095 --> 00:19:58.345
It's almost a zen-like,
you have to take the ego out of ideas,

00:19:58.345 --> 00:20:00.484
which is a very hard thing to do.

00:20:00.484 --> 00:20:02.359
&gt;&gt; Absolutely.

00:20:02.359 --> 00:20:03.357
You never went to business school.

00:20:03.357 --> 00:20:06.338
You're in an audience full of folks who,
obvious, are in business school.

00:20:06.338 --> 00:20:10.455
What do you hope that
we leave here knowing?

00:20:10.455 --> 00:20:14.805
Both about the Valley and technology
that you learned from actual experience?

00:20:14.805 --> 00:20:21.045
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so the big thing,
it all sounds so easy, from a distance.

00:20:21.045 --> 00:20:23.037
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; It all sounds so straight forward,

00:20:23.037 --> 00:20:24.246
like the case makes it all so clear.

00:20:24.246 --> 00:20:30.147
So, strategy is important.

00:20:30.147 --> 00:20:31.286
Strategy is thinking about like,

00:20:31.286 --> 00:20:33.888
okay what are the decisions that should
be made over time, is very important.

00:20:33.888 --> 00:20:36.630
I like to come at it, particularly,
in terms of running something,

00:20:36.630 --> 00:20:38.137
like running a company.

00:20:38.137 --> 00:20:40.093
Making strategic decisions
that affect your company.

00:20:40.093 --> 00:20:42.957
The strategic decisions sound
more obvious than they are, and

00:20:42.957 --> 00:20:46.157
one is there's just a lot of
context at the time that's missing.

00:20:46.157 --> 00:20:47.577
Even in the histories
it's usually missing.

00:20:47.577 --> 00:20:50.892
The other thing I think, the biggest thing
I find newly minted MBAs have a hard

00:20:50.892 --> 00:20:54.003
time wrapping their heads around is,
the abstract idea of what the right

00:20:54.003 --> 00:20:56.502
thing to do is in a business
situation is all well and good,

00:20:56.502 --> 00:20:59.841
but as an actual manager in an actual
business, you have other constraints.

00:20:59.841 --> 00:21:03.560
In particular,
you have organizational constraints.

00:21:03.560 --> 00:21:06.661
You have constraints as to what
your actual organization can do.

00:21:06.661 --> 00:21:10.070
And those constraints have to
do with your employee base,

00:21:10.070 --> 00:21:15.151
how well your company's organized and run,
the existing commitments you've made to

00:21:15.151 --> 00:21:20.740
those employees, the expectations of your
investors, the expectations of your board.

00:21:20.740 --> 00:21:24.740
Andy Grove had this sort of way the he
described it, which is basically he said,

00:21:24.740 --> 00:21:25.933
as the CEO's in place,

00:21:25.933 --> 00:21:29.654
basically on day one a CEO has complete
latitude to do whatever they want.

00:21:29.654 --> 00:21:30.966
On day two that's no longer true,

00:21:30.966 --> 00:21:33.420
because you've started to
make commitments to people.

00:21:33.420 --> 00:21:35.901
You've started to tell the board,
okay here's my plan, I'm going to do x.

00:21:35.901 --> 00:21:38.130
You told investors,
here's my plan, I'm going to do x.

00:21:38.130 --> 00:21:40.796
You've told employees, here's what
we're going to do, here's the plan.

00:21:40.796 --> 00:21:45.075
So then as a CEO, you get 6 months in, 12
months in, 18 months in, 24 months in, and

00:21:45.075 --> 00:21:46.860
you change your view on strategy.

00:21:46.860 --> 00:21:50.130
And you go back to all the people and
you tell them we're going to do something

00:21:50.130 --> 00:21:53.559
different and they all say, but you just
got done telling us the opposite last

00:21:53.559 --> 00:21:56.715
week, or last month, or last quarter,
like what's wrong with you?

00:21:56.715 --> 00:22:00.620
And so, basically, what happens is
the people who take the idealized approach

00:22:00.620 --> 00:22:05.250
of shifting strategies when it seems
logical, typically end up getting fired.

00:22:05.250 --> 00:22:09.450
They literally end up getting either fired
explicitly, their board will fire them or

00:22:09.450 --> 00:22:12.030
their investors will fire them, because
they appear that they're lurching all over

00:22:12.030 --> 00:22:14.300
the place and
whip-lashing the organization.

00:22:14.300 --> 00:22:16.714
Or they get fired,
they just get abandoned, right?

00:22:16.714 --> 00:22:20.748
Like the employees and the investors all
get a vote, and they get to leave and

00:22:20.748 --> 00:22:21.520
they quit.

00:22:21.520 --> 00:22:24.006
I'll have this conversation sometimes
with founders where I'm like,

00:22:24.006 --> 00:22:25.064
that's all well and good, but

00:22:25.064 --> 00:22:27.722
like look around you, you're going to
be sitting here by yourself, right?

00:22:27.722 --> 00:22:29.332
How do you feel about that?

00:22:29.332 --> 00:22:31.990
With this big new decision that
you think you're going to make.

00:22:31.990 --> 00:22:35.208
And so, the real world
constraints are very intense, and

00:22:35.208 --> 00:22:39.281
they're very context specific to that
company, at that moment in time,

00:22:39.281 --> 00:22:41.394
and kind of how it got to where it got to.

00:22:41.394 --> 00:22:44.309
Now, Andy's response to that
was very crisp and solid,

00:22:44.309 --> 00:22:46.750
which was basically,
he literally said this.

00:22:46.750 --> 00:22:49.384
He's like, well okay, if the next
guy is going to come in here and

00:22:49.384 --> 00:22:52.270
he's going to do all these decisions,
why don't we just go outside and

00:22:52.270 --> 00:22:54.608
come back in through the door and
we'll just go ahead and

00:22:54.608 --> 00:22:58.084
we'll just breach all the old commitments
and we'll do the clearly right thing.

00:22:58.084 --> 00:23:02.072
And so, you're the leader, you're
responsible for it, the answer has to be,

00:23:02.072 --> 00:23:04.370
you have to be able to
work your way through it.

00:23:04.370 --> 00:23:06.856
You have to be able to break
the glass to be able to do that.

00:23:06.856 --> 00:23:10.500
It's just that's much harder and
riskier than it seems from the outside.

00:23:10.500 --> 00:23:13.981
&gt;&gt; Speaking of context in leadership,
you ran a very successful company with

00:23:13.981 --> 00:23:17.684
technical employees, and now you run a
very successful VC with a lot of partners

00:23:17.684 --> 00:23:20.300
who were successful entrepreneurs
in their own right.

00:23:20.300 --> 00:23:23.748
What are the differences in leadership
you have to display in both those

00:23:23.748 --> 00:23:26.517
environments that you've
learned to bounce, I guess?

00:23:26.517 --> 00:23:30.600
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so the big thing,
Siri is weighting in.

00:23:30.600 --> 00:23:33.730
Siri has opinions on management
here in the front row.

00:23:33.730 --> 00:23:38.772
So, the big thing as an operator, what I
always found as an operator, it's like

00:23:38.772 --> 00:23:44.060
a 90, 10 thing, where you're spending
90% of your time making decisions.

00:23:44.060 --> 00:23:47.691
Like 90% of the time you're sitting
there and customers, and issues, and

00:23:47.691 --> 00:23:50.952
you're getting employee product reviews,
and employee feedback.

00:23:50.952 --> 00:23:54.927
And you're making hiring decisions, firing
decisions, market expansion decisions,

00:23:54.927 --> 00:23:58.760
financial decisions, and so it's just an
unending series of decisions all day long.

00:23:58.760 --> 00:24:01.470
And then you get maybe 10% of
your time to sit and think.

00:24:01.470 --> 00:24:04.100
If you're lucky and
if you're disciplined you carve out 10%.

00:24:04.100 --> 00:24:07.470
You kind of think and read, and
get caught up on kind of your own.

00:24:07.470 --> 00:24:08.500
Your own theories on things.

00:24:08.500 --> 00:24:09.886
And you kind of have to do that,

00:24:09.886 --> 00:24:12.492
because these markets tend to
be brutally competitive, and

00:24:12.492 --> 00:24:15.940
if you just sit around thinking,
your company's not going to go anywhere.

00:24:17.320 --> 00:24:19.940
As an investor, and anybody who's
been an investor will tell you,

00:24:19.940 --> 00:24:23.150
if you take that approach to investing,
you'll blow yourself to bits, right?

00:24:23.150 --> 00:24:25.232
If your approach to
investing is 90% action,

00:24:25.232 --> 00:24:28.680
10% thought, right, then you're like
every other schmo in the stock market.

00:24:28.680 --> 00:24:31.380
You're just churning through stocks and
you're going to, ultimately,

00:24:31.380 --> 00:24:32.650
as a stock market investor,

00:24:32.650 --> 00:24:35.540
you're going to do terribly because
you're going to churn your portfolio.

00:24:35.540 --> 00:24:37.290
As a VC, you're just going to
blow yourself to bits.

00:24:37.290 --> 00:24:39.610
You'll do the first ten deals and then
that's it and then they'll all fail and

00:24:39.610 --> 00:24:41.420
you're done, your career's over.

00:24:41.420 --> 00:24:44.870
And so a biased transaction as
an investor is a very dangerous thing,

00:24:44.870 --> 00:24:46.400
generally a very bad thing.

00:24:46.400 --> 00:24:51.070
And so, the great VCs and the great,
by the way, great VCs, the great public

00:24:51.070 --> 00:24:55.640
market managers, the great hedge fund
managers, anybody with long term horizon.

00:24:56.810 --> 00:25:01.174
They seem to spend 90% of their time
thinking, thinking and arguing.

00:25:01.174 --> 00:25:04.846
There's thinking and then there's the
actual process of all the people around

00:25:04.846 --> 00:25:07.309
you having to try to convince
them of your crazy idea so

00:25:07.309 --> 00:25:09.086
the arguing part ends up dominating.

00:25:09.086 --> 00:25:12.881
So 90% of your time arguing and
10% of your time, or maybe even less,

00:25:12.881 --> 00:25:13.930
making decisions.

00:25:13.930 --> 00:25:15.190
One of the things we ask ourselves is,

00:25:15.190 --> 00:25:18.010
how many decisions a year do
we make that actually matter?

00:25:18.010 --> 00:25:20.773
And it's probably 20?

00:25:20.773 --> 00:25:24.344
It's probably the new investments we make,
which is about 20 a year and

00:25:24.344 --> 00:25:25.449
that's probably it.

00:25:25.449 --> 00:25:28.972
And there's lots of things we advise on
and help with, but the decisions we make,

00:25:28.972 --> 00:25:31.170
it's probably two a month, at max.

00:25:31.170 --> 00:25:34.330
And so, it's a radically
different balance of time.

00:25:35.700 --> 00:25:39.020
By the way, if you've in an operating job,
it sounds very appealing because you don't

00:25:39.020 --> 00:25:41.860
have to sit there all day long and
be involved in all this activity and

00:25:41.860 --> 00:25:44.990
you get all this time to think and read
and talk to people and it sounds great.

00:25:44.990 --> 00:25:49.110
Except a lot of operators
are highly action-oriented and

00:25:49.110 --> 00:25:52.430
their entire success in their career
has come from taking action right?

00:25:52.430 --> 00:25:53.720
Crisply and aggressively.

00:25:53.720 --> 00:25:56.555
And so a lot of operators can't make
the jump to ever being an investor.

00:25:56.555 --> 00:25:57.930
because it's just too frustrating, right?

00:25:57.930 --> 00:26:00.570
Six months in, they're just
like crawling the walls, right?

00:26:00.570 --> 00:26:03.645
They'll go home at the end of the day and
they're literally,

00:26:03.645 --> 00:26:05.551
I didn't do anything today, right?

00:26:05.551 --> 00:26:08.237
This day was a total write off,
I made no decisions, right?

00:26:08.237 --> 00:26:13.518
And that's true of about 28 out
of 30 days kind of by definition.

00:26:13.518 --> 00:26:17.310
And so I'd say most, I'm sorry, most
investors can't make the jump operator,

00:26:17.310 --> 00:26:19.397
because they would turn
into a little puddle.

00:26:19.397 --> 00:26:22.300
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Of plasma under their desk.

00:26:22.300 --> 00:26:26.100
Most operators can't become investors,

00:26:26.100 --> 00:26:28.340
because they can't deal with
the frustration of not taking the action.

00:26:28.340 --> 00:26:31.230
And so we're trying to straddle
it somewhere in the middle.

00:26:31.230 --> 00:26:35.560
This is the speech I give when
we only have former founders or

00:26:35.560 --> 00:26:37.100
CEOs as GPs at our firm.

00:26:37.100 --> 00:26:39.751
So we have all been through this
transition, and this is the little speech

00:26:39.751 --> 00:26:42.593
that I give every time, and they all
tell me, yep, you got it, I understand.

00:26:42.593 --> 00:26:46.835
And then six months later,
they're like, I see.

00:26:46.835 --> 00:26:47.955
&gt;&gt; So you mentioned crazy ideas.

00:26:47.955 --> 00:26:51.569
What are some of those crazy ideas that
you guys are banting around in the A16Z

00:26:51.569 --> 00:26:54.801
offices that you think will define
the future of the Silicon Valley and

00:26:54.801 --> 00:26:55.373
tech here?

00:26:55.373 --> 00:27:03.140
&gt;&gt; Yeah, the way we sort of go is we call
ourselves extremists thinking about ideas.

00:27:03.140 --> 00:27:06.600
Generally, smart people in the industry
are going to have figured out everything

00:27:06.600 --> 00:27:08.150
that's going to happen the next five or
ten years.

00:27:09.750 --> 00:27:12.360
It's one of the things like,
[LAUGH] Peter,

00:27:12.360 --> 00:27:15.240
my little argument with my shoulder Peter,
Peter talks a lot about secrets.

00:27:15.240 --> 00:27:17.730
And he talks about secrets being
something that you know or

00:27:17.730 --> 00:27:20.680
believe that other people either
don't know or don't believe.

00:27:20.680 --> 00:27:22.670
And I actually think like over a five or
ten year period,

00:27:22.670 --> 00:27:24.480
there actually aren't very many secrets.

00:27:24.480 --> 00:27:26.898
Most of the good ideas
are already kind of obvious.

00:27:26.898 --> 00:27:29.838
And by the way, they've probably been
tried before and they probably failed, but

00:27:29.838 --> 00:27:31.611
the time is going to come
where they're going to work.

00:27:31.611 --> 00:27:34.993
And so we in the industry,
the iPad is a spectacular new idea,

00:27:34.993 --> 00:27:36.627
except it wasn't a new idea.

00:27:36.627 --> 00:27:38.835
Apple had something called
the Newton 20 years earlier,

00:27:38.835 --> 00:27:40.214
but it was basically the same thing.

00:27:40.214 --> 00:27:42.950
It failed then, it worked in 2009.

00:27:42.950 --> 00:27:44.580
So tablet computing was not a new idea,

00:27:44.580 --> 00:27:46.770
it just happened to take
20 years to get it to work.

00:27:46.770 --> 00:27:50.890
But for that entire 20 year period,
it did look like a good new idea.

00:27:50.890 --> 00:27:53.000
So most of the good ideas
are kind of out there.

00:27:53.000 --> 00:27:53.820
They're circulating or,

00:27:53.820 --> 00:27:56.600
by the way, they're running in labs,
over here in the engineering department.

00:27:56.600 --> 00:27:57.250
They're already up and

00:27:57.250 --> 00:28:00.290
running as prototypes and
grad students are working on them.

00:28:00.290 --> 00:28:05.080
So that part of it is not, and we swim in
that world and trying to understand it and

00:28:05.080 --> 00:28:05.740
all that, but

00:28:05.740 --> 00:28:09.610
it's not that there are huge undiscovered
ideas with a short term timeframe.

00:28:09.610 --> 00:28:13.162
So I think we spend a lot more of our time
on the idea side, trying to think, okay,

00:28:13.162 --> 00:28:14.438
ten plus years out, right?

00:28:14.438 --> 00:28:18.024
Sort of take the extreme view, right,
of okay, it's sort of a [INAUDIBLE] of

00:28:18.024 --> 00:28:21.459
the venture philosophy, which is,
forget whether or not it will work.

00:28:21.459 --> 00:28:23.632
Ask the question about
what if it did work, and

00:28:23.632 --> 00:28:26.920
then kind of push that question
out as far as you can, right?

00:28:26.920 --> 00:28:28.230
So I mentioned self-driving cars.

00:28:28.230 --> 00:28:31.060
So let's just assume for a moment
that self-driving cars actually work.

00:28:31.060 --> 00:28:33.550
Okay, what are the consequences of that?

00:28:33.550 --> 00:28:35.490
Well, for example, one consequence is,

00:28:35.490 --> 00:28:39.430
potentially, cars changed our
idea of geography, right?

00:28:39.430 --> 00:28:42.180
Before cars, everybody used
to have to live in the city.

00:28:42.180 --> 00:28:44.240
Cars created the idea of a suburb, right?

00:28:44.240 --> 00:28:46.410
because you could actually commute.

00:28:46.410 --> 00:28:48.002
We all sit here 80 years later,

00:28:48.002 --> 00:28:52.390
wishing that nobody had thought of
that because commutes are horrible.

00:28:52.390 --> 00:28:55.880
The number one correlator to job
satisfaction is commute time, right?

00:28:55.880 --> 00:28:58.000
And so, you see this every day.

00:28:58.000 --> 00:29:00.072
You're sitting there in traffic,
sitting there next to,

00:29:00.072 --> 00:29:01.860
and so everybody has the same epiphany.

00:29:01.860 --> 00:29:03.930
It's like, it's eight in the morning,
I'm sitting there, and I'm like, okay,

00:29:03.930 --> 00:29:05.650
I'm sitting, there's car over
there with one person in it,

00:29:05.650 --> 00:29:06.920
there's a car over here
with one person in it.

00:29:06.920 --> 00:29:08.220
And I'm sitting here along.

00:29:08.220 --> 00:29:10.870
Like somebody should really
come up with a car pooling app.

00:29:10.870 --> 00:29:15.126
Every morning, right, every driver has
that idea and we all still commute, right,

00:29:15.126 --> 00:29:15.784
in our cars.

00:29:15.784 --> 00:29:18.215
And said we all hate it, and
so, and it's all wasted time.

00:29:18.215 --> 00:29:22.348
And we have people at our companies now
who commute two or three hours a day, and

00:29:22.348 --> 00:29:24.400
it's just completely wasted time.

00:29:24.400 --> 00:29:26.150
I mean,
now they can play Pokemon Go, right,

00:29:26.150 --> 00:29:29.580
while they drive, but
that has other issues.

00:29:30.690 --> 00:29:32.140
So then he said, okay, self-driving cars.

00:29:32.140 --> 00:29:35.190
Well, self-driving cars,
maybe you can reclaim all that time.

00:29:35.190 --> 00:29:36.800
Maybe you can reclaim all
the commute time, right?

00:29:36.800 --> 00:29:40.607
And so maybe all of a sudden you can have
the idea that maybe an hour long commute

00:29:40.607 --> 00:29:42.199
is actually a big perk, right?

00:29:42.199 --> 00:29:45.453
Because instead of driving,
instead of having to sit and focus and

00:29:45.453 --> 00:29:49.133
merge through traffic, what if your
car is a rolling living room, right?

00:29:49.133 --> 00:29:52.541
And what if you get to spend that
hour playing with your kid or

00:29:52.541 --> 00:29:55.966
reading the news or watching TV or
actual working, right?

00:29:55.966 --> 00:29:58.046
Because you don't have
to worry about driving.

00:29:58.046 --> 00:30:00.050
Or what if you had
different kinds of cars?

00:30:00.050 --> 00:30:01.520
What if some cars are rolling offices?

00:30:01.520 --> 00:30:06.070
What if I can take a nap, I could sleep.

00:30:06.070 --> 00:30:08.440
I can sleep for six hours at home,
get in the car, sleep for another hour and

00:30:08.440 --> 00:30:10.660
a half on my way to work,
and be all set, right?

00:30:10.660 --> 00:30:15.250
And so in that version of the world,
all of a sudden, maybe suburbs,

00:30:15.250 --> 00:30:17.450
like maybe now we go to exurbs.

00:30:17.450 --> 00:30:20.130
Maybe now geography becomes actually
much more protractible, and

00:30:20.130 --> 00:30:22.672
we can have these urban
environments get much, much larger.

00:30:22.672 --> 00:30:24.880
And then we're trying to think,
okay, then back from that,

00:30:24.880 --> 00:30:26.660
what would be the consequences of that?

00:30:26.660 --> 00:30:29.561
What would be the consequences of that
in terms of how these company get built?

00:30:29.561 --> 00:30:32.710
What would be the infrastructure that
has to get built to support that

00:30:32.710 --> 00:30:33.425
kind of thing?

00:30:33.425 --> 00:30:36.660
What are the kind of early signals that
kind of show that that kind of thing is

00:30:36.660 --> 00:30:38.180
either starting to happen or not.

00:30:38.180 --> 00:30:41.062
And then try to chart at least some
view of how the future will unfold.

00:30:41.062 --> 00:30:42.911
And so, yeah.

00:30:42.911 --> 00:30:46.240
&gt;&gt; So, you mentioned a lot of us have
great ideas we want to execute on.

00:30:46.240 --> 00:30:50.992
You've also noted that capital is
abundant and opportunities are scarce.

00:30:50.992 --> 00:30:54.652
And I think venture capitals are only
a $50 billion industry amongst

00:30:54.652 --> 00:30:56.550
a multi-trillion dollar economy.

00:30:56.550 --> 00:30:59.388
What do you make of the different
investment choices being made by larger

00:30:59.388 --> 00:31:01.029
companies vice what's happening here?

00:31:01.029 --> 00:31:05.142
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so this is the big thing,
I think, about tech.

00:31:05.142 --> 00:31:08.600
[LAUGH] l So, once upon a time,
we had a tech bubble.

00:31:08.600 --> 00:31:11.540
Some of you may have noticed or
read about it.

00:31:11.540 --> 00:31:15.705
It was followed by
a catastrophic crash in 2000.

00:31:15.705 --> 00:31:21.539
It was an actual bubble,
the numbers are very clear on that.

00:31:21.539 --> 00:31:24.441
There was about four years of nuclear
winter, where very little happened, and

00:31:24.441 --> 00:31:26.532
then starting in 2004,
tech started to work again.

00:31:26.532 --> 00:31:31.352
And between 2004 and today, it's just been
constant commentary of bubble, bubble,

00:31:31.352 --> 00:31:32.048
new bubble.

00:31:32.048 --> 00:31:35.656
Tech bubble 2.0,
all the way up and to the right,

00:31:35.656 --> 00:31:39.106
as tech has worked constant
screaming of bubble.

00:31:39.106 --> 00:31:41.240
Up to and including the chairman
of the Federal Reserve.

00:31:41.240 --> 00:31:43.310
Janet Yellen has developed
a theory that tech's in a bubble,

00:31:43.310 --> 00:31:45.265
which we really appreciate out here.

00:31:45.265 --> 00:31:48.300
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I think she might have slightly larger

00:31:48.300 --> 00:31:50.230
issues to deal with.

00:31:51.800 --> 00:31:54.750
And by the way, with imminent
predictions of a crash, right?

00:31:54.750 --> 00:31:56.030
Which hasn't happened, right?

00:31:56.030 --> 00:31:59.674
And it's every six months, imminent
predictions of a crash, no crash, and

00:31:59.674 --> 00:32:01.987
it might actually be
starting to give up on this.

00:32:01.987 --> 00:32:05.936
Which ironically would probably
be the sign of an actual bubble.

00:32:05.936 --> 00:32:08.957
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; In any event,

00:32:08.957 --> 00:32:12.761
the result of all that is
exactly what you said.

00:32:12.761 --> 00:32:17.378
So all of new tech in the US,
all of new tech venture capital, growth,

00:32:17.378 --> 00:32:22.258
investing, all of what we would consider
to be what Silicon Valley does.

00:32:22.258 --> 00:32:24.867
Total capital in is about
$50 billion a year.

00:32:24.867 --> 00:32:28.367
And again, sounds like a big number,
is a big number on an absolute basis.

00:32:28.367 --> 00:32:31.908
It's a drop in the bucket from
a national or global standpoint.

00:32:31.908 --> 00:32:36.859
So the US economy, the US economy is,
I don't know, like $6 trillion or

00:32:36.859 --> 00:32:40.885
something like that and
then just look at financial assets.

00:32:40.885 --> 00:32:45.259
I think the number is now it's like
$14 trillion of negative yielding

00:32:45.259 --> 00:32:47.133
government debt in the world.

00:32:47.133 --> 00:32:51.777
So bonds that people have to pay for
the privilege of holding and

00:32:51.777 --> 00:32:55.204
then just even looking
in corporate America.

00:32:55.204 --> 00:32:59.282
This year alone corporate America will
discourage more than a trillion dollars of

00:32:59.282 --> 00:33:00.711
cash back to shareholders.

00:33:00.711 --> 00:33:03.746
Let's just think about it this way, like a
trillion dollars in cash was coming out of

00:33:03.746 --> 00:33:06.613
the Fortune 500 that literally can't
figure out anything to do with the cash

00:33:06.613 --> 00:33:08.837
other than give it back,
anything productive to do with it.

00:33:08.837 --> 00:33:10.694
So they give it back,
dividends and buyback.

00:33:10.694 --> 00:33:13.375
So a trillion dollars comes out and
then 50 billion,

00:33:13.375 --> 00:33:15.309
basically of that goes back into tech.

00:33:15.309 --> 00:33:18.363
Where does the other 950 billion go,
right?

00:33:18.363 --> 00:33:19.862
Into negative yielding bonds.

00:33:19.862 --> 00:33:25.481
And so which is what, to me, it's like
okay, why are interest rates so low?

00:33:25.481 --> 00:33:28.181
I think interest rates are so
low for a fundamental reason,

00:33:28.181 --> 00:33:29.968
which is the world is awash in capital.

00:33:29.968 --> 00:33:32.027
Basically, the 20th century worked.

00:33:32.027 --> 00:33:35.158
The 20th century created enormous amounts
of wealth all around the world and

00:33:35.158 --> 00:33:36.207
that process continues.

00:33:36.207 --> 00:33:41.278
And now that money is all out there,
sitting in these giant pools.

00:33:41.278 --> 00:33:43.298
Primarily, people's retirement savings.

00:33:43.298 --> 00:33:46.403
These huge pensions or these big sovereign
wealth funds that basically represent

00:33:46.403 --> 00:33:47.618
people's future retirement.

00:33:47.618 --> 00:33:51.870
And that money, all needs to earn a return
of 6 or 7 or 8% per year to pay for

00:33:51.870 --> 00:33:53.212
everybody's old age.

00:33:53.212 --> 00:33:56.949
And that money is seeking out
investment opportunities and

00:33:56.949 --> 00:34:01.293
it can put 50 billion into tech, and
then where else is it going to go?

00:34:01.293 --> 00:34:03.961
And so
I not don't think there's a tech bubble,

00:34:03.961 --> 00:34:06.052
I think there's a massive tech bust.

00:34:06.052 --> 00:34:09.841
I think we're running a critical shortage
of new technology in the world and I think

00:34:09.841 --> 00:34:13.575
you can view through a technological lens
in productivity growth, but I think you

00:34:13.575 --> 00:34:16.955
can also view that on a financial lens,
which is there literally are not.

00:34:16.955 --> 00:34:21.016
We as a society cannot come up with enough
places to productively deploy capital and

00:34:21.016 --> 00:34:21.944
this is the thing,

00:34:21.944 --> 00:34:25.381
like this just seems completely
bizarre like do we have any problems?

00:34:25.381 --> 00:34:26.240
Yes, we do.

00:34:26.240 --> 00:34:29.375
We've got massive education problems,
massive healthcare problems.

00:34:29.375 --> 00:34:32.057
There are big problems all over
the way still to this day.

00:34:32.057 --> 00:34:34.426
[LAUGH] Funny problems are funny.

00:34:34.426 --> 00:34:36.353
We use to have a massive
global hunger problem.

00:34:36.353 --> 00:34:38.791
Now of course,
we have a massive global obesity problem.

00:34:38.791 --> 00:34:42.448
Good news, people aren't starving
to death as much anymore.

00:34:42.448 --> 00:34:46.435
Bad news, now everybody's going to die
of diabetes and heart attacks, but

00:34:46.435 --> 00:34:47.987
like it's still a problem.

00:34:47.987 --> 00:34:50.018
So now,
we have a big problem on the other side.

00:34:50.018 --> 00:34:51.818
And so, we've got the money.

00:34:51.818 --> 00:34:54.508
Through most of recorded history,
you'd have these problems and

00:34:54.508 --> 00:34:56.439
you wouldn't be able to
find an answer to them.

00:34:56.439 --> 00:34:59.155
So we've got the money,
we can fund the answers.

00:34:59.155 --> 00:35:01.853
We've got the problems,
we know what we have to go do.

00:35:01.853 --> 00:35:04.106
It's the process in the middle
that's kind of all screwed up.

00:35:04.106 --> 00:35:07.302
And so, that's where I just think like
a tax on Silicon Valley like being bad at

00:35:07.302 --> 00:35:09.446
innovation or
whatever is kind of beside the point.

00:35:09.446 --> 00:35:12.778
Even if you're right, even if the valley,
even if the critics are all right and

00:35:12.778 --> 00:35:16.159
a tax on Silicon Valley is a joke and it's
all just a bunch of photo sharing apps and

00:35:16.159 --> 00:35:20.232
self-driving cars will never happen and
we're also it's like okay, so then what?

00:35:20.232 --> 00:35:22.415
If like if we suck, okay, fine.

00:35:22.415 --> 00:35:24.609
Like who else is going to do it?

00:35:24.609 --> 00:35:26.711
By the way, the Chinese are going for it.

00:35:26.711 --> 00:35:31.216
But again there, the dollar there's a big
tech investment happening in China.

00:35:31.216 --> 00:35:34.776
But again, the absolute dollar is just not
that relative to even the Chinese economy,

00:35:34.776 --> 00:35:35.768
much less our economy.

00:35:35.768 --> 00:35:41.782
And so, I think it's a tech depression
more than it's a tech bubble.

00:35:41.782 --> 00:35:45.450
&gt;&gt; Is that risk aversion on the parts of
investors or is it rational behavior?

00:35:45.450 --> 00:35:46.472
&gt;&gt; Well, it's a combination.

00:35:46.472 --> 00:35:47.552
It's a bunch of factors.

00:35:47.552 --> 00:35:49.180
I think like for sure,

00:35:49.180 --> 00:35:54.251
we've been on a risk reverse equity
market since the 2000 crash.

00:35:54.251 --> 00:35:56.775
If you look at the history of crashes and

00:35:56.775 --> 00:35:59.765
what happens after people
fight the last war.

00:35:59.765 --> 00:36:02.146
And so, what we know from
2000 is that stocks crash.

00:36:02.146 --> 00:36:04.061
So, everyone's constantly fearful.

00:36:04.061 --> 00:36:06.286
The headlines are always about
the market's about to crash,

00:36:06.286 --> 00:36:07.022
like all the way up.

00:36:07.022 --> 00:36:12.084
And then since 2008, on top of that,
we now have a massive aversion to debt.

00:36:12.084 --> 00:36:16.137
We're incredibly scared about debt.

00:36:16.137 --> 00:36:17.656
We're scared about real estate.

00:36:17.656 --> 00:36:18.574
It's really interesting.

00:36:18.574 --> 00:36:20.854
You talk to like our investors or
some of these big,

00:36:20.854 --> 00:36:23.520
like the Stanford endowment
here manages $25 billion.

00:36:23.520 --> 00:36:27.027
And if you talk to folks like the guys
who run the Stanford endowment not

00:36:27.027 --> 00:36:29.419
them specifically, but
that class of people.

00:36:29.419 --> 00:36:31.415
It's just like, they literally,
they go through.

00:36:31.415 --> 00:36:34.819
Here are all the things we can
invest in like public equities,

00:36:34.819 --> 00:36:39.799
terrible performance, 2008 crash horrible
bonds, we just had a financial crisis.

00:36:39.799 --> 00:36:42.725
Bonds are largely unsafe and
don't yield anything, anyway.

00:36:42.725 --> 00:36:44.362
Real estate, blew up in 2008.

00:36:44.362 --> 00:36:45.825
That can blow up at any moment.

00:36:45.825 --> 00:36:48.818
Commodities, it looked like oil
was going to go to the Moon.

00:36:48.818 --> 00:36:51.198
Now, looks like the world's
awash in an oil glut.

00:36:51.198 --> 00:36:53.636
The Saudis are dumping on
the market at any price.

00:36:53.636 --> 00:36:55.506
So, you can't invest in commodities.

00:36:55.506 --> 00:36:59.257
Hedge funds,
activist hedge funds were working.

00:36:59.257 --> 00:37:01.300
And now, they don't work any more and
they're blowing up.

00:37:01.300 --> 00:37:04.693
Venture capitals, we hear good things,
but everybody says, it's a joke.

00:37:04.693 --> 00:37:07.781
So maybe we can only put a small
amount of money in it, anyway.

00:37:07.781 --> 00:37:10.060
And so, they just literally
like a process of elimination.

00:37:10.060 --> 00:37:12.522
It's like, okay,
where is the money going to go?

00:37:12.522 --> 00:37:16.775
And so it's this weirdly perverse like
it's like I get really pessimistic about

00:37:16.775 --> 00:37:20.731
this, because I'm like the transmission
mechanism is not working properly.

00:37:20.731 --> 00:37:24.019
Like somehow, there's something
between the money, the problem and

00:37:24.019 --> 00:37:27.142
then the transmission mechanism to
connect the money to the problem

00:37:27.142 --> 00:37:30.559
that involves ideas, people,
companies, technology, management.

00:37:30.559 --> 00:37:34.135
Something in there is broken,
but I also get very optimistic.

00:37:34.135 --> 00:37:37.023
because it's like boy, if we could figure
out how to get that transmission mechanism

00:37:37.023 --> 00:37:38.905
working better,
we could go do much bigger things.

00:37:38.905 --> 00:37:40.942
And that,
by the way is a very big reason for

00:37:40.942 --> 00:37:44.395
optimism in the valley as you do have
a lot of founders in the valley now who

00:37:44.395 --> 00:37:48.445
are going after much bigger problems
that people used to go after.

00:37:48.445 --> 00:37:53.145
Like it did not use to be the case that
the valley would go after ideas like just

00:37:53.145 --> 00:37:56.410
with the huge effort, it's not going on to
buffer different areas of healthcare or

00:37:56.410 --> 00:37:58.630
transportation as an example or
even space.

00:37:59.986 --> 00:38:01.274
With SpaceX.

00:38:01.274 --> 00:38:04.792
It was just inconceivable that those
ideas would have been tried 20 years ago.

00:38:04.792 --> 00:38:08.279
And so, there are a group of people who
do have this view that we can now go do

00:38:08.279 --> 00:38:09.022
these things.

00:38:09.022 --> 00:38:11.680
&gt;&gt; I could sit here for hours.

00:38:11.680 --> 00:38:14.621
Unfortunately, I have time for one last
question before Q&amp;A from the audience and

00:38:14.621 --> 00:38:15.721
this is deeply personal to me.

00:38:15.721 --> 00:38:18.983
I know you have a 20-month old son,
I have a 22-month old son.

00:38:18.983 --> 00:38:21.293
How should I raise him in the next five,

00:38:21.293 --> 00:38:24.103
ten years to be relevant
in the 21st century?

00:38:24.103 --> 00:38:29.547
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Can I get back to you in [LAUGH] 20 or

00:38:29.547 --> 00:38:33.300
25 years once we find out
how it went on our end?

00:38:33.300 --> 00:38:36.979
Look, I think curiosity.

00:38:36.979 --> 00:38:38.267
I mean, I just think curiosity.

00:38:38.267 --> 00:38:40.467
I think it's a cliche,
but I think it's true,

00:38:40.467 --> 00:38:44.271
which is just whatever the assumptions
are about how the world's working today.

00:38:44.271 --> 00:38:48.671
So many kids just get brought up,
I think Douglass Adams had this old line.

00:38:48.671 --> 00:38:54.931
He said, whatever existed before you were
born is just like ancient and irrelevant.

00:38:54.931 --> 00:38:58.958
Whatever happens when you are,
whatever happens, new technologies,

00:38:58.958 --> 00:39:01.210
new ideas happen when you're an adult.

00:39:01.210 --> 00:39:05.994
When you're a young adult are exciting and
fresh and whenever ideas that

00:39:05.994 --> 00:39:11.014
appear past the age of 50 are against
the holy order of things and it's this

00:39:11.014 --> 00:39:16.127
natural kind of human kind of view of
like it's weird, history is very long.

00:39:16.127 --> 00:39:17.671
We show up a bunch of stuff has happened.

00:39:17.671 --> 00:39:20.050
We draw a bunch of conclusions from that,
a bunch of other stuff happens.

00:39:20.050 --> 00:39:22.338
We have a relatively narrow
window to have an impact on that.

00:39:22.338 --> 00:39:27.093
And so I think that curiosity just is
really, really critical curiosity and

00:39:27.093 --> 00:39:28.442
an ability to learn.

00:39:28.442 --> 00:39:32.228
Frankly, everyone has
an opinion on education.

00:39:32.228 --> 00:39:36.452
My opinion is our education system is
still dominated by industrial age theory

00:39:36.452 --> 00:39:38.567
of we're creating cogs for a machine.

00:39:38.567 --> 00:39:42.743
I mean, luckily at places like Stanford,
there are exceptions to that, but

00:39:42.743 --> 00:39:46.725
a lot of K through 12 a lot of that is
fundamentally to get people to work on

00:39:46.725 --> 00:39:48.031
farms or in factories.

00:39:48.031 --> 00:39:50.049
And if you trace the ideas
behind education,

00:39:50.049 --> 00:39:53.501
people sitting in rows in a classroom and
somebody that's a senior authority

00:39:53.501 --> 00:39:56.014
figure sitting in front lecturing and
the whole thing.

00:39:56.014 --> 00:40:00.196
That's very kind of 1890
kind of era philosophy.

00:40:00.196 --> 00:40:01.494
Frederic, what's his name?

00:40:01.494 --> 00:40:04.257
It's mass production.

00:40:04.257 --> 00:40:04.780
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:40:04.780 --> 00:40:06.150
&gt;&gt; Applied to education.

00:40:06.150 --> 00:40:09.280
And so I think that the thing
with kids is to try to get them

00:40:09.280 --> 00:40:11.980
To the point where curiosity and
learning is good and exciting and fun.

00:40:13.060 --> 00:40:16.490
Right, and it sounds obvious, and yet,
for most people, that never happens.

00:40:16.490 --> 00:40:19.252
&gt;&gt; Yeah, well, let me turn it over
to the audience for some questions.

00:40:28.774 --> 00:40:29.515
&gt;&gt; Hi, Mark.

00:40:29.515 --> 00:40:33.780
This is Sam, 2015 alumni.

00:40:33.780 --> 00:40:37.591
I've seen the ecosystem for
the startup VC.

00:40:37.591 --> 00:40:41.930
Have changed, evolved significantly
over the past ten years,

00:40:41.930 --> 00:40:45.940
in terms more entrepreneurship,
educational resources,

00:40:45.940 --> 00:40:49.920
cheaper resources for start-ups,
and the more incubator startups.

00:40:49.920 --> 00:40:55.800
So, that probably make the competition,
amount of VC's more serious nowadays.

00:40:55.800 --> 00:40:59.850
So, I'm curious about what's your view
about this ecosystem over the next five,

00:40:59.850 --> 00:41:00.400
ten years?

00:41:00.400 --> 00:41:04.100
And what matters most to to in this world.

00:41:04.100 --> 00:41:07.310
Is it your vision, your PR marketing, or

00:41:07.310 --> 00:41:11.472
your disciplines on the research and
due diligence?

00:41:11.472 --> 00:41:14.040
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so the big thing I think,
from what you said,

00:41:14.040 --> 00:41:17.520
that I agree with you that the startup
ecosystem has changed a lot.

00:41:17.520 --> 00:41:18.770
And generally, in a very positive way.

00:41:18.770 --> 00:41:22.860
The big thing that's happened, as you
said, the big thing that's happened is

00:41:22.860 --> 00:41:25.520
the education is so
much more widely available.

00:41:25.520 --> 00:41:28.390
There are now so many more people, both
here in the valley and around the world,

00:41:28.390 --> 00:41:31.565
who now know how to start a company.

00:41:31.565 --> 00:41:35.765
And that, again, maybe sounds obvious,
but when I arrived in the valley in 1994,

00:41:35.765 --> 00:41:37.305
I went to the book store, and

00:41:37.305 --> 00:41:39.415
I tried to find a book on
how to start a tech company.

00:41:39.415 --> 00:41:41.531
And that was a long and
lonely trip through the bookshelves.

00:41:41.531 --> 00:41:44.565
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; There was very little.

00:41:44.565 --> 00:41:45.995
Luckily, Andy's book was there, but

00:41:45.995 --> 00:41:48.485
Andy's book was about how to run Intel,
which, it was a monopoly.

00:41:48.485 --> 00:41:52.225
I couldn't really get a lot of benefit
out of that for my startup at the time.

00:41:53.610 --> 00:41:56.930
And so, the resources just
simply didn't exist, right?

00:41:56.930 --> 00:41:59.070
And today you've got, it's everything.

00:41:59.070 --> 00:42:00.830
A lot of it comes out of Standford.

00:42:00.830 --> 00:42:05.865
Just the CS-183 course at Stanford
that various people have taught,

00:42:05.865 --> 00:42:08.230
Peter taught, Simon has taught.

00:42:08.230 --> 00:42:11.550
And then the fact that those classes are
not just accessible to people at Stanford,

00:42:11.550 --> 00:42:13.000
they're accessible to people
all around the world.

00:42:13.000 --> 00:42:15.310
And there are people all around
the world watching, right?

00:42:15.310 --> 00:42:18.380
Those videos all the time,
learning how to start companies.

00:42:18.380 --> 00:42:20.420
And so, the information's out, and

00:42:20.420 --> 00:42:23.340
you have these new ecosystem kind
of participants, like Y combinator,

00:42:23.340 --> 00:42:25.900
in particular, which has had
this big impact in the valley.

00:42:25.900 --> 00:42:30.570
Y Combinator is now producing 240
startups a year in the Bay Area out

00:42:30.570 --> 00:42:33.650
of their current program, and
they're planning to ramp that up.

00:42:35.650 --> 00:42:39.888
Ironically, for venture capital,
this has all been a tremendous blessing,

00:42:39.888 --> 00:42:41.438
because this is steel flow.

00:42:41.438 --> 00:42:44.095
And so, the more startups there are,

00:42:44.095 --> 00:42:47.508
the pond that we fish out
of is very well stocked.

00:42:47.508 --> 00:42:51.083
So, we are super, super thrilled
that all these things are happening,

00:42:51.083 --> 00:42:54.657
because we get to evaluate a far larger
number of startups per year than used to

00:42:54.657 --> 00:42:56.100
be the case.

00:42:56.100 --> 00:43:00.376
I think the competition is actually
much more intense between the startups.

00:43:00.376 --> 00:43:03.299
And you actually see that,
how many you'll see that, where there will

00:43:03.299 --> 00:43:06.366
be a company that will be super hot in
a YC class that we'll be doing an idea,

00:43:06.366 --> 00:43:10.046
and then they'll be the belle of the ball,
and they'll raise money, and it's all bad.

00:43:10.046 --> 00:43:11.859
And then six months later,
in the next class,

00:43:11.859 --> 00:43:14.149
there'll be another company
doing the exact same thing.

00:43:14.149 --> 00:43:17.752
[LAUGH] And now they're the hot company,
and the previous company's, what the hell?

00:43:17.752 --> 00:43:19.282
And then they compete.

00:43:19.282 --> 00:43:22.610
And then they both come to raise
venture capital a year later.

00:43:22.610 --> 00:43:24.650
And then we, as VCs,
get to pick between the two.

00:43:24.650 --> 00:43:28.980
And so that's, at least so far,
it's been really great for VC.

00:43:28.980 --> 00:43:32.620
If you're thinking about
starting a company, therefore,

00:43:32.620 --> 00:43:33.850
really stresses an idea,

00:43:33.850 --> 00:43:37.650
and again, I'll use my Peter here on
the shoulder, thing channel this through.

00:43:37.650 --> 00:43:41.230
Something Peter says I think is really
relevant, which is, a lot of founders

00:43:41.230 --> 00:43:43.765
think about what it takes to get
the second person into the company,

00:43:43.765 --> 00:43:47.345
the second founder or the second engineer,
or the third person or the fourth person.

00:43:47.345 --> 00:43:48.775
He says that's no longer the challenge.

00:43:48.775 --> 00:43:51.645
Everyone wants to start a company, and so,
everybody can get together two or three or

00:43:51.645 --> 00:43:52.645
four people to start a company.

00:43:52.645 --> 00:43:55.929
He said the challenge is how do
you get engineer number 20, right?

00:43:55.929 --> 00:43:57.522
Because engineer number 20, right?

00:43:57.522 --> 00:43:59.095
If they're in this ecosystem, right?

00:43:59.095 --> 00:44:00.815
They could go start their own company.

00:44:00.815 --> 00:44:03.232
And so, how can you convince
somebody to join your company's,

00:44:03.232 --> 00:44:06.340
essentially, you're number 20 with
a tiny percentage of equity, right?

00:44:06.340 --> 00:44:09.870
And not being in charge, as compared
to starting their own company, and

00:44:09.870 --> 00:44:11.690
getting the equity and being in charge.

00:44:11.690 --> 00:44:13.900
Now, one of the things to do is you
tell them they're going to have

00:44:13.900 --> 00:44:15.010
the same problem.

00:44:15.010 --> 00:44:16.930
But then they respond to you that
you also have that problem, and

00:44:16.930 --> 00:44:21.700
then you sit there and
stare at each other awkwardly.

00:44:21.700 --> 00:44:24.410
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So, that's the thing.

00:44:24.410 --> 00:44:29.000
So, what you have right now is you just
have a very large number of seed funded

00:44:29.000 --> 00:44:31.103
companies that are experiments.

00:44:31.103 --> 00:44:33.609
And they're experiments on their ideas,
but more importantly,

00:44:33.609 --> 00:44:36.753
they're experiments on who has the will
and the drive and the horsepower, right?

00:44:36.753 --> 00:44:39.980
And the salesmanship and everything
else that goes into recruiting, right?

00:44:39.980 --> 00:44:42.860
And I think actually Sam Altman,
again, I talked to the other day.

00:44:42.860 --> 00:44:44.220
He said, if you could wave a magic wand,

00:44:44.220 --> 00:44:47.310
you would combine a lot of these companies
together to get critical mass, but

00:44:47.310 --> 00:44:50.140
mergers between startups almost never
happen for a variety of reasons.

00:44:50.140 --> 00:44:53.055
And so, it makes this sort of acute,

00:44:53.055 --> 00:44:55.855
this recruiting problem
is a very acute problem.

00:44:55.855 --> 00:44:59.435
By the way, the temptation is to think
about it as a recruiting problem,

00:44:59.435 --> 00:45:00.945
which is we have to get
really good at recruiting.

00:45:00.945 --> 00:45:06.915
But it's not really that as much as it's
like, okay, what about my company is going

00:45:06.915 --> 00:45:11.105
to be so spectacular, and so special,
and so unusual, and so distinct and

00:45:11.105 --> 00:45:14.490
differentiated that it's going to be
able to easily hire engineer number 20?

00:45:14.490 --> 00:45:16.750
Away from starting his or her own company.

00:45:16.750 --> 00:45:19.402
And so, to me,
it basically translates into basically,

00:45:19.402 --> 00:45:20.710
you just have to get better.

00:45:20.710 --> 00:45:22.583
You have to be able to compete, and

00:45:22.583 --> 00:45:25.508
you have to win in this
really brutal initial battle.

00:45:25.508 --> 00:45:31.025
The good news is the companies coming out
of that sort of churning kind of, sort of

00:45:31.025 --> 00:45:34.315
snakepit competition, the companies
that come out of that are really strong,

00:45:34.315 --> 00:45:36.465
because they had to be to come through it.

00:45:36.465 --> 00:45:37.378
But it is tougher, and

00:45:37.378 --> 00:45:40.128
it is the other side of how easy
it's become to start the companies.

00:45:43.467 --> 00:45:48.535
&gt;&gt; [SOUND] Thanks, Mark,

00:45:48.535 --> 00:45:52.008
for your time.

00:45:52.008 --> 00:45:57.120
I have a question about
the international investing.

00:45:57.120 --> 00:46:01.980
So, I see some on top VCs
like Saquioya Perkins,

00:46:01.980 --> 00:46:05.720
they went to China market many years ago.

00:46:05.720 --> 00:46:12.870
But in the contrast, I think, and just
pretty much focusing on the US market.

00:46:12.870 --> 00:46:17.960
I want to your point of view
of expending investing in

00:46:17.960 --> 00:46:23.080
those eMarkets like China India,

00:46:23.080 --> 00:46:27.460
South East Asia,
just like to hear about your view.

00:46:28.680 --> 00:46:31.180
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so, it's very,
it's extremely tempting.

00:46:31.180 --> 00:46:33.220
Because they're entrepreneurial
opportunities and

00:46:33.220 --> 00:46:34.590
great companies all over the world.

00:46:34.590 --> 00:46:37.330
Increasingly distributed,
right, all over the world.

00:46:37.330 --> 00:46:39.300
Literally, and so it's very tempting.

00:46:39.300 --> 00:46:41.380
But I'll tell you the typical problem, and

00:46:41.380 --> 00:46:44.910
then I'll tell you the theoretical
question that comes out the other side.

00:46:44.910 --> 00:46:46.280
So, the problem is,

00:46:46.280 --> 00:46:50.050
to the extent that venture capital
investing is like we understand it today,

00:46:50.050 --> 00:46:53.510
which is it's a very hands on process
of really deeply understanding.

00:46:53.510 --> 00:46:54.510
It's a people business.

00:46:54.510 --> 00:46:56.380
It's a process of very deeply
understanding the people

00:46:56.380 --> 00:46:57.420
you are working with.

00:46:57.420 --> 00:46:58.560
Deeply understanding the founders,

00:46:58.560 --> 00:47:01.090
deeply understanding the executives
in the company, the engineers,

00:47:01.090 --> 00:47:03.260
the management team, the culture.

00:47:03.260 --> 00:47:06.800
You both have to evaluate the company and
also to help work with the company.

00:47:06.800 --> 00:47:09.090
It has always been a hands on
business like that, right?

00:47:09.090 --> 00:47:09.640
When it's worked.

00:47:09.640 --> 00:47:12.880
So, if it continues to be
a hands on business like that,

00:47:12.880 --> 00:47:15.350
then there's a problem with
geographical remoteness, right?

00:47:15.350 --> 00:47:19.040
Which is,
if I'm not present in another geography,

00:47:19.040 --> 00:47:21.980
do I really know the people well
enough to make those decisions?

00:47:21.980 --> 00:47:24.110
So, then, what a bunch of firms
are trying to do is they then staff

00:47:24.110 --> 00:47:25.630
local teams, right?

00:47:25.630 --> 00:47:29.110
But then you have this fundamental problem
where you have this selection problem,

00:47:29.110 --> 00:47:33.120
where if that local team is really good,
they can easily leave and

00:47:33.120 --> 00:47:36.720
go run their own firm,
which is actually the exactly

00:47:36.720 --> 00:47:40.330
same problem I talked about with startups,
it also applies to venture capital firms.

00:47:40.330 --> 00:47:42.577
Say we had a really good
team on the ground in China,

00:47:42.577 --> 00:47:45.106
how can I convince them to stay
with us as opposed to just?

00:47:45.106 --> 00:47:48.136
Because they could very easily go down
the street open up their own shop,

00:47:48.136 --> 00:47:48.740
raise money.

00:47:48.740 --> 00:47:51.390
There's plenty of investors in China
who want to invest in venture capital.

00:47:51.390 --> 00:47:54.190
And so, if they're good,
they leave and start their own firm.

00:47:54.190 --> 00:47:56.764
If they're bad, they stay working for me.

00:47:56.764 --> 00:47:59.930
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Which has its own issues.

00:47:59.930 --> 00:48:03.120
And so,
most of those experiments have not worked.

00:48:03.120 --> 00:48:06.110
It is very striking, Sequoia in
particular, has worked really well.

00:48:06.110 --> 00:48:07.330
And they have built a very good team, and

00:48:07.330 --> 00:48:09.812
they figured out a very
good dynamic on that.

00:48:09.812 --> 00:48:13.300
But it is a very tough question,
because you have to really dig into, okay,

00:48:13.300 --> 00:48:16.669
why did it work for them, and
why has it not worked for so many others,

00:48:16.669 --> 00:48:18.965
if you want to think about
doing that yourself.

00:48:18.965 --> 00:48:24.397
And so, that's a live topic, or
question, in the venture ecosystem.

00:48:24.397 --> 00:48:26.941
Now, there's the assumption,
go back to first principles,

00:48:26.941 --> 00:48:30.255
the assumption underneath that,is that
venture capital is a hands on business.

00:48:30.255 --> 00:48:32.160
And should be a hands on business,
the way I've described it, right?

00:48:32.160 --> 00:48:36.490
And again, if you look at history, if you
look at investment banks 100 years ago,

00:48:36.490 --> 00:48:38.180
they were very hands on like this.

00:48:38.180 --> 00:48:42.626
If you look at private equity firms in the
70s, when they were a boutique business,

00:48:42.626 --> 00:48:44.441
they were very hands on like this.

00:48:44.441 --> 00:48:48.378
At one point stock investing
was probably a lot like this.

00:48:48.378 --> 00:48:51.147
These days, in a lot of fields
it's not hands on anymore,

00:48:51.147 --> 00:48:54.664
now it's fully become computerized,
systematized and computerized.

00:48:54.664 --> 00:48:59.159
And so, the counterargument to my whole
theory on this is I'm describing venture

00:48:59.159 --> 00:49:01.059
capital the old-fashioned way.

00:49:01.059 --> 00:49:04.141
It shouldn't be so based on people, it
should be done through algorithms, right?

00:49:04.141 --> 00:49:06.402
You should be able to do
quantitative venture capital, right?

00:49:06.402 --> 00:49:09.376
And so you should be able to either like
open up a crowd funding marketplace,

00:49:09.376 --> 00:49:10.861
and let the world compete with ideas,

00:49:10.861 --> 00:49:14.270
and to be able to raise capital,
which is what AngieList is trying to do.

00:49:14.270 --> 00:49:18.588
Or you should, there's various firms now
trying to do quantitative venture capital.

00:49:18.588 --> 00:49:21.426
You should basically go gather all
the data, you can look at everybody's

00:49:21.426 --> 00:49:24.312
college transcripts, you can look
at everybody's past career records,

00:49:24.312 --> 00:49:26.910
their LinkedIn connections, or
whatever data you want to look at.

00:49:26.910 --> 00:49:31.079
And you can assemble a quantitative
view to predict success or failure,

00:49:31.079 --> 00:49:32.317
and invest that way.

00:49:32.317 --> 00:49:34.908
People are trying,
I would say we haven't yet

00:49:34.908 --> 00:49:38.701
gotten to the point where we think
we've figured out how to do that.

00:49:38.701 --> 00:49:42.416
Again, I fantasize that somebody
is going to come in and

00:49:42.416 --> 00:49:47.742
pinch us on building that as a company and
we can fund it, and then we can retire.

00:49:47.742 --> 00:49:50.612
I think, that question of is
it really going to continue to

00:49:50.612 --> 00:49:54.197
be that hands on is the question
underneath the question that you asked.

00:49:54.197 --> 00:49:55.689
I think it's an open question.

00:50:03.141 --> 00:50:04.870
&gt;&gt; Hi, my name's Sam Jackson.

00:50:04.870 --> 00:50:08.538
A question about the question
of capital wash.

00:50:08.538 --> 00:50:11.160
So, what advice would you have for

00:50:11.160 --> 00:50:13.590
people who have big ideas that
might be very capital intensive.

00:50:13.590 --> 00:50:15.321
And a couple of years
ago I asked Elon Musk,

00:50:15.321 --> 00:50:17.918
what was his advice if someone
started a new SpaceX or Tesla, and

00:50:17.918 --> 00:50:20.340
he suggested that I become
an internet billionaire first.

00:50:20.340 --> 00:50:21.591
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So,

00:50:21.591 --> 00:50:24.015
I was wondering if you had any other,
maybe, easier ideas.

00:50:24.015 --> 00:50:30.900
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; That is how he did it.

00:50:30.900 --> 00:50:34.959
So, it is actually striking,
it is striking.

00:50:34.959 --> 00:50:38.693
The guys who are most prone to be like,
yeah, this software stuff is useless and

00:50:38.693 --> 00:50:41.369
stupid, and we should be going
to space travel instead,

00:50:41.369 --> 00:50:45.140
they all started doing the useless and
stupid software stuff, so that is true.

00:50:46.180 --> 00:50:51.896
I would like to tell you, again,
I'll word this as a question.

00:50:51.896 --> 00:50:53.915
I'll pose an open question.

00:50:53.915 --> 00:50:55.266
Which we're trying to think through, and

00:50:55.266 --> 00:50:56.886
I think a lot of people
are trying to think through.

00:50:56.886 --> 00:50:59.254
Here's how I think about this which is,
okay.

00:50:59.254 --> 00:51:02.854
There's a well known corporate investing
model which is GE or Honeywell.

00:51:02.854 --> 00:51:06.004
You go to one of those places or Lockheed,
and they've got their big programs, and

00:51:06.004 --> 00:51:08.344
you either work the bureaucracy
to do your program or not, and

00:51:08.344 --> 00:51:10.880
that's where most of these big
capital intensive things happen.

00:51:10.880 --> 00:51:13.682
And then there's the venture
capital model, and

00:51:13.682 --> 00:51:17.498
the venture capital model is sort
of staging the capital over time.

00:51:17.498 --> 00:51:20.526
It's milestone based investing, but
the milestones are kind of undefined, and

00:51:20.526 --> 00:51:22.630
then you're going to a new
investor in every round.

00:51:22.630 --> 00:51:26.470
And so you've kind of got this constant
financing risk, which says well,

00:51:26.470 --> 00:51:27.740
what if I can't raise
the next round of money?

00:51:27.740 --> 00:51:30.922
And then you've got the problem with these
capital intensive projects where it's like

00:51:30.922 --> 00:51:33.505
well, I know it's going to take a half
a billion dollars to get to market.

00:51:33.505 --> 00:51:35.542
If I go raise $20 million from VCs today,

00:51:35.542 --> 00:51:39.080
I have this massive funding risk of
$480 million in the future, right?

00:51:39.080 --> 00:51:40.060
And then from your standpoint,

00:51:40.060 --> 00:51:42.390
can I even recruit the team to
be able to build the product?

00:51:42.390 --> 00:51:44.620
Because I can't even tell them
that the money will be there.

00:51:44.620 --> 00:51:45.947
And then Andrisa stood on stage and

00:51:45.947 --> 00:51:48.878
told me about the $14 billion of
negative yielding government debt, and

00:51:48.878 --> 00:51:51.698
why can't I get somebody to give me
the $500 billion to do the thing.

00:51:51.698 --> 00:51:55.973
And so, the idea that's floating
around is sort of an idea to borrow

00:51:55.973 --> 00:51:59.545
an idea from actually a large
public infrastructure.

00:51:59.545 --> 00:52:02.980
And private infrastructure gets built,
which is project finance,

00:52:02.980 --> 00:52:05.416
which is basically like
the way a dam gets built.

00:52:05.416 --> 00:52:10.394
Which is basically the way a lot of
airplane projects get run, new airplanes,

00:52:10.394 --> 00:52:13.412
is basically,
like the Silicon Valley model,

00:52:13.412 --> 00:52:17.750
we're going to have a new idea,
we're also going to have milestones.

00:52:17.750 --> 00:52:20.170
Unlike the Silicon Valley model,
we're going to have a very,

00:52:20.170 --> 00:52:22.690
very crisp understanding of what
those milestones are, right?

00:52:22.690 --> 00:52:25.740
We're going to have a program management
office that's at a level of sophistication

00:52:25.740 --> 00:52:27.920
that a typically valley
start-up doesn't have.

00:52:27.920 --> 00:52:31.557
And we're going to chart out, in detail,
how that money is going to get spent,

00:52:31.557 --> 00:52:33.462
what the milestones are along the way,

00:52:33.462 --> 00:52:36.023
how the organization's
going to get built to do that.

00:52:36.023 --> 00:52:38.591
And it's going to be precise,
we're going to know week by week what

00:52:38.591 --> 00:52:41.170
all the work is, and all the parts,
and how they fit together.

00:52:41.170 --> 00:52:44.570
And we're going to try to get to
a much higher level of predictability

00:52:44.570 --> 00:52:46.860
to build the thing than a typical
Valley start-up would have.

00:52:46.860 --> 00:52:50.385
And then in theory, you could then
raise money, and this is sort

00:52:50.385 --> 00:52:54.970
of where there could be a new asset class,
and call it like, tech project finance.

00:52:54.970 --> 00:52:58.466
Where you could basically say, okay you
could go and you could raise $500 million,

00:52:58.466 --> 00:53:01.076
and the $500 million wouldn't
show up in your bank account.

00:53:01.076 --> 00:53:03.921
Instead enough money to get to
the first milestone would show up, and

00:53:03.921 --> 00:53:07.403
then there'd be a checkpoint, and then if
you hit the checkpoint you get the money,

00:53:07.403 --> 00:53:09.240
right, and so the money unlocks over time.

00:53:09.240 --> 00:53:13.091
And so, then you could think about
having a sort of tech project finance

00:53:13.091 --> 00:53:17.020
venture fund, where you could have
like 20 projects like that start.

00:53:17.020 --> 00:53:19.840
And then you could assume that like
10 of them fail along the way and

00:53:19.840 --> 00:53:21.260
get liquidated, right?

00:53:21.260 --> 00:53:23.897
And just get shut down before
most of the money is gone in.

00:53:23.897 --> 00:53:25.284
And maybe 10 of them work and

00:53:25.284 --> 00:53:28.514
then the fund is big enough to
fund 10 to completion, but not 20.

00:53:28.514 --> 00:53:30.630
And you kind of play it
like a venture portfolio.

00:53:30.630 --> 00:53:33.070
So, that idea is kind of
floating out there.

00:53:33.070 --> 00:53:34.770
And by the way,
this is what I'm describing.

00:53:34.770 --> 00:53:37.660
This is kind of how Google X works
a little bit, and this is how

00:53:37.660 --> 00:53:40.420
some of the advanced RNB projects, and
some of big value companies, operate.

00:53:40.420 --> 00:53:41.895
And so, that idea's out there, but

00:53:41.895 --> 00:53:44.235
it doesn't fully exist in
the form that I just described.

00:53:44.235 --> 00:53:47.773
And I think one of the really interesting
questions for the future of tech is,

00:53:47.773 --> 00:53:50.616
are we collectively going to figure
out a way to do that, right?

00:53:50.616 --> 00:53:52.274
because if we can't we can't
figure out a way to do that,

00:53:52.274 --> 00:53:55.318
then the project's you're describing
either have to be these flukes, right?

00:53:55.318 --> 00:53:57.814
You have a once in a generation Elon Musk,
Internet Billionaire,

00:53:57.814 --> 00:54:00.459
who decides to build a space company and
a car company at the same time.

00:54:00.459 --> 00:54:01.615
because who would do that?

00:54:01.615 --> 00:54:04.500
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Or it's just going to be big companies

00:54:04.500 --> 00:54:06.640
doing all this stuff, right,
which would be a depressing answer.

00:54:08.260 --> 00:54:09.450
Mark, thank you so much for your time.

00:54:09.450 --> 00:54:10.414
We appreciate you being here.

00:54:10.414 --> 00:54:12.022
&gt;&gt; Good, good,
thanks everybody, thank you.

00:54:12.022 --> 00:54:15.960
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

